      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to analyze the shape and geometry of the sectors and the quadrilateral formed by joining the endpoints of the sectors.\n\n1. **Understanding the Sectors:**\n   Each sector of the circle has a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). The radius of the circle is \\(12\\).\n\n2. **Area of One Sector:**\n   The area of a sector of a circle is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius.\n\n   For one sector with a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) and radius \\(12\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n   \\]\n\n3. **Area of the Quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\):**\n   Since the two sectors are placed side by side, the quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is a rectangle with height equal to the length of the arc of one sector and width equal to the diameter of the circle.\n\n   - The length of the arc of one sector is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Arc length} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times 2\\pi r = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times 2\\pi \\times 12 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times 24\\pi = 4\\pi\n     \\]\n   - The width of the rectangle is the diameter of the circle:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Diameter} = 2r = 2 \\times 12 = 24\n     \\]\n\n   Therefore, the area of the rectangle \\(ABCD\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of rectangle} = \\text{Length} \\times \\text{Width} = 4\\pi \\times 24 = 96\\pi\n   \\]\n\n4. **Final Answer:**\n   The area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is the area of the rectangle, which is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{96\\pi}\n   \\]",
