      "text": "To determine the area of figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to analyze the given information and break down the problem into manageable steps.\n\n1. **Identify the Geometry of the Sector:**\n   Each sector has a radius of \\(12\\) and a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). The area \\(A\\) of a sector of a circle with radius \\(r\\) and central angle \\(\\theta\\) (in degrees) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\pi r^2\n   \\]\n   For our sector, \\(r = 12\\) and \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\pi (12)^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\pi (144) = 24\\pi\n   \\]\n   So, the area of one sector is \\(24\\pi\\).\n\n2. **Determine the Area of the Rectangle:**\n   The figure \\(ABCD\\) consists of two sectors placed side by side, forming a rectangle with a width equal to the length of the arc of each sector and a height equal to the radius of the circle. The length of each arc is the length of the chord that subtends a \\(60^\\circ\\) angle at the center of the circle. This chord can be calculated using the formula for the chord length of a circle:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Chord length} = 2r \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   For our sectors, the chord length is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Chord length} = 2 \\times 12 \\times \\sin\\left(\\frac{60^\\circ}{2}\\right) = 24 \\sin(30^\\circ) = 24 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 12\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the width of the rectangle is \\(12\\). The height of the rectangle is the radius of the circle, which is \\(12\\). The area of the rectangle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of rectangle} = \\text{width} \\times \\text{height} = 12 \\times 12 = 144\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the Total Area:**\n   The total area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is the sum of the areas of the two sectors and the area of the rectangle:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area} = \\text{Area of one sector} + \\text{Area of rectangle} = 24\\pi + 144\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{144 + 24\\pi}\\).",
